---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
// @ts-expect-error This Node-only import is executed during Astro static generation; this browser-focused project does not ship Node types.
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
// Render the unchanged public source record at build time: readers must be
// able to inspect it without JavaScript or a second network request.
const originalSourceNotes = readFileSync('public/examples/claim-boundary-source-notes.txt','utf8');
---
<Base title="Source Claim Verifier: 22 Evidence Checks" description="Practice 22 source-fidelity cases, then build and download your own human-reviewed claim ledger without sharing source text with a server." breadcrumbs={[{name:'Free Tools',url:'/tools/'},{name:'Source Claim Verifier',url:'/tools/claim-checker/'}]}>
  <header class="page-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow mint">/ tools / verify</p>
    <h1>Does the source actually support that claim?</h1>
    <p>Practice the difference between a contradicted statement and an unknown statement. Classify editor-written claims against the original notes, see the reasoning, and try again. No account, AI subscription, or uploads.</p>
  </header>
  <section class="prose info-content">
    <h2>What makes this different from asking an AI to check itself</h2>
    <p>We supply the source, the claim, the reference answer, and the exact reason for that answer. You can independently read the fictional records before checking the published key. An AI assistant does not judge your choices and nothing in the quiz suggests a model was benchmarked.</p>
    <p><strong>SUPPORTED</strong> means the evidence establishes the claim. <strong>CONTRADICTED</strong> means it says something inconsistent. <strong>UNSUPPORTED</strong> means the claim is not established either way. Missing evidence is not proof that a statement is false.</p>
    <p>Start with five cases to see the pattern; select all 22 when ready. The exercise is based on the same public <a href="/examples/claim-boundary-source-notes.txt">source notes</a> and <a href="/examples/claim-boundary-audit.csv">complete answer-key CSV</a> used in our <a href="/evidence/">Evidence Library</a>. The data is fictional and was created for teaching, not from real users or a live AI experiment.</p>
  </section>
  <div class="tool-workspace">
    <section class="tool-form" aria-labelledby="source-notes-title">
      <h2 id="source-notes-title">Step 1 · Read the source notes</h2>
      <p class="tool-footnote">The complete original fictional notes are printed below even when JavaScript is disabled or the grading file cannot load. Use these notes—not assumptions about a real venue, product, or event.</p>
      <pre class="claim-audit-source" id="claim-source-notes" tabindex="0">{originalSourceNotes}</pre>
      <p><a href="/examples/claim-boundary-source-notes.txt">Open the original source notes as text →</a></p>
      <p class="tool-footnote">The source material is editorially invented and contains no personal records. Your answers remain in this browser page. There is no AI service call or scoring server.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="tool-output" aria-labelledby="audit-quiz-title">
      <h2 id="audit-quiz-title">Step 2 · Classify the claims</h2>
      <form id="claim-audit-form">
        <label for="claim-audit-length">Choose a practice set
          <select id="claim-audit-length" disabled>
            <option value="sample">Five essential cases</option>
            <option value="all">All 22 original cases</option>
          </select>
        </label>
        <div id="claim-audit-questions" class="claim-audit-questions">
          <p>Loading the public exercise.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tool-actions">
          <button class="primary" id="claim-audit-submit" type="submit" disabled>Check my source-reading answers</button>
          <button type="button" id="claim-audit-reset" disabled>Try again</button>
        </div>
      </form>
      <p id="claim-audit-status" class="tool-status" role="status" aria-live="polite">Loading the editorial case set.</p>
      <div id="claim-audit-results" class="claim-audit-feedback" aria-live="polite"></div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="personal-ledger-title">
    <header class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow mint">/ your_own_source_review</p><h2 id="personal-ledger-title">Build a claim ledger from your own text</h2></div></header>
    <p>Use this as a <strong>manual source-review worksheet</strong> after the fictional quiz. Paste a claim, identify the document you checked, record the exact supporting or contradicting passage, and choose your own verdict. Add multiple rows and download one TSV audit register. This does <strong>not</strong> check whether your source is genuine or whether your verdict is correct. No AI account or server upload is involved.</p>
    <div class="tool-workspace">
      <form id="personal-claim-form" class="tool-form">
        <fieldset disabled aria-label="Manual claim review fields">
          <label for="personal-source">Source title or reference (required)
            <input id="personal-source" name="source" required maxlength="500" placeholder="For example: approved meeting notes, section 2" />
          </label>
          <label for="personal-claim">Claim to check (required)
            <textarea id="personal-claim" name="claim" required maxlength="2000" rows="2" placeholder="For example: The venue is confirmed."></textarea>
          </label>
          <label for="personal-passage">Exact passage from the source (required for supported/contradicted)
            <textarea id="personal-passage" name="passage" maxlength="3000" rows="3" placeholder="Paste the exact wording you inspected; leave blank only if the source does not address the claim."></textarea>
          </label>
          <label for="personal-verdict">Your review verdict (required)
            <select id="personal-verdict" name="verdict" required>
              <option value="">Choose a verdict</option>
              <option value="SUPPORTED">SUPPORTED — supported by the source</option>
              <option value="CONTRADICTED">CONTRADICTED — inconsistent with the source</option>
              <option value="UNSUPPORTED">UNSUPPORTED — not established either way</option>
            </select>
          </label>
          <label for="personal-note">Why you chose that verdict (required)
            <textarea id="personal-note" name="note" required maxlength="3000" rows="3" placeholder="Explain which condition, exception, number, or missing evidence affects your conclusion."></textarea>
          </label>
          <div class="tool-actions"><button class="primary" id="personal-ledger-save" type="submit">Add reviewed claim</button><button type="reset">Clear current fields</button><button type="button" id="personal-ledger-cancel-edit" disabled>Cancel edit</button></div>
        </fieldset>
      </form>
      <section class="tool-output" aria-labelledby="personal-ledger-results-title">
        <h3 id="personal-ledger-results-title">Your local review register</h3>
        <p id="personal-ledger-status" class="tool-status" role="status" aria-live="polite">The manual worksheet requires JavaScript. No information is submitted.</p>
        <ol id="personal-ledger-entries" class="personal-ledger-entries"></ol>
        <div class="tool-actions"><button type="button" id="personal-ledger-download" disabled>Download my TSV</button><button type="button" id="personal-ledger-clear" disabled>Clear all reviews</button></div>
        <p class="tool-footnote">Maximum 30 reviews per page session. Select Edit beside a review to correct it without losing its reference ID; Cancel edit preserves the original row. Clear all reviews also erases unfinished form fields. The download button requests a file but cannot confirm your browser saved it. No auto-save or account sync. Download the TSV before leaving. Do not enter private or confidential data on a shared device. Spreadsheet formula-like text is exported as text. Tabs, newlines and backslashes are escaped so source content cannot create extra audit rows and each original escape is distinguishable.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
    <div class="prose info-content">
      <h3>A one-minute source check you can reproduce</h3>
      <p>In the published <a href="/examples/claim-boundary-source-notes.txt">fictional garden notes</a>, the meeting location is explicitly unconfirmed. Enter the narrower claim “The meeting will be held at North Greenhouse,” identify the fictional notes as your source, choose <strong>UNSUPPORTED</strong>, and explain that the notes do not establish the named venue. The source rules out treating any location as <em>confirmed</em>, but does not establish which location will eventually be chosen. If you instead claim “The meeting location has already been confirmed,” mark it <strong>CONTRADICTED</strong>: the notes explicitly say the location is not confirmed.</p>
      <p>For a different claim, “Offline mode was approved,” the fictional release note says it <em>has not been approved</em>. Record that exact passage and choose <strong>CONTRADICTED</strong>. Add both rows, export, and check the two judgments and reasons in the resulting TSV. This is a manual traceability exercise, not evidence that a model has verified your claims.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <noscript><p>The personal review form is disabled without JavaScript so it cannot accidentally send notes in a GET request. You can still write a manual table with Source, Claim, Exact Passage, Verdict, and Reason and save it privately.</p></noscript>
  <noscript><p>The original source notes above remain readable without JavaScript. Automatic questions and scoring require JavaScript; download the <a href="/examples/claim-boundary-audit.csv">22-case answer key</a> to compare claims manually. You can also <a href="/examples/claim-boundary-source-notes.txt">open the source notes as text</a>.</p></noscript>
  <section class="section prose info-content">
    <h2>Try the verification without a loading indicator</h2>
    <p>Before answering, read the notes displayed in Step 1; they are part of the page itself and do not depend on an external fetch. The interactive questions and reference verdicts still use the <a href="/examples/claim-boundary-audit.csv">separate 22-case CSV</a>. If that file is temporarily unavailable or you have disabled scripts, you can still read the same source notes above, open the CSV manually, and compare claims using the published editorial explanations. Nothing about a successful page load proves the verdicts are objectively true.</p>
    <h2>Three errors this practice can expose</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Confusing an unknown with a false claim:</strong> the garden notes do not confirm a meeting location. That does not prove that a particular location is impossible.</li>
      <li><strong>Promoting a proposal into a fact:</strong> the software notes say offline mode has not been approved. A claim that it is an approved feature directly contradicts the source.</li>
      <li><strong>Adding a hidden assumption to correct arithmetic:</strong> three bags at $8 and two trays at $6 total $36 for supplies, but the notes never establish delivery cost. A subtotal is not evidence of free delivery.</li>
    </ol>
    <h2>A useful second attempt</h2>
    <p>Change one fact in a copy of the fictional source notes—such as moving the garden meeting from Saturday to Sunday. Reconsider the affected verdicts without changing unrelated facts. A change to the meeting date does not establish the location, alter a product's release approval, or change the supplies subtotal.</p>
    <h2>How we verified this quiz on the live website</h2>
    <p>On November 1, 2026, we actually selected all five answers in the public browser version and inspected the checked options before clicking the grading button. With G04 deliberately labeled SUPPORTED (incorrect) and G06, G09, R02 and E06 set to the editorial reference, the displayed result was <strong>4 of 5 matched</strong>. The case-by-case feedback identified G04 alone as incorrect because the meeting location was not confirmed. This is a browser observation, separate from the automated scoring tests.</p>
    <p><a href="https://agent.tinyfish.ai/runs/e6804798-a2cd-4872-9283-a2c5761d5391">See the actual browser run</a> and <a href="/examples/firsthand-browser-qa-2026-11-01.json">review the exact choices and outcomes</a>. An earlier browser attempt had reported an ambiguous 1/5 without recorded checked inputs; the new complete run verifies the current path but does not establish why that earlier attempt differed.</p>
    <h2>Why the reference key is not a benchmark</h2>
    <p>The 22 cases and explanations were written by GuideHollow Editorial. Scores on this page compare <em>your selections</em> with those published explanations; they are not claims about an AI model's reliability, and they do not prove whether a real-world statement is true. For high-stakes material, verify the actual source and seek qualified review when appropriate.</p>
    <p>The <a href="https://github.com/emfls/guidehollow/blob/main/tests/claim-checker.test.mjs">automated fixture test</a> checks the completeness and scoring behavior of this tool, and the <a href="/guides/how-to-verify-ai-generated-information/">verification guide</a> explains how to investigate outside sources. If an editorial answer looks wrong, <a href="/contact/">report the precise case ID and supporting passage</a>.</p>
  </section>
  <style>
    .claim-audit-source{max-height:34rem;overflow:auto;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere;font-size:.78rem;line-height:1.6;background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:1rem;color:var(--text)}
    .claim-audit-questions{display:grid;gap:1.2rem;margin:1.2rem 0}
    .claim-audit-question{padding:1rem;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;min-width:0}
    .claim-audit-question legend{font-weight:650;font-size:.85rem}
    .claim-audit-question p{margin:.5rem 0 1rem}
    .claim-audit-choices{display:grid;gap:.55rem}
    .claim-audit-choices label{display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:.5rem;font-size:.88rem;cursor:pointer}
    .claim-audit-choices input{margin-top:.3rem;min-width:auto}
    .claim-audit-feedback{margin-top:1.6rem}
    .claim-audit-feedback li{margin-bottom:.7rem}
    #claim-audit-length{display:block;width:100%;margin-top:.5rem;padding:.6rem}
  </style>
  <script>import '../../scripts/claim-checker'; import '../../scripts/custom-claim-ledger';</script>
</Base>
